From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 00:37:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: BF16 partial sums on the AFD return wire for
 prompt windows, TF_GLM_PARTIALS_BF16 (glm53f-afd's BF16 return planes)

Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden) authored the BF16 return path of glm53f-afd (@ 91db3cc,
crates/glm53f-rank/README.md: the large-M path's reduce step and the return-path table, "Four BF16 planes, added in
FP32 by the coordinator"): each expert rank sums its routed slots in FP32, rounds the sum to BF16 and returns it, and
the coordinator adds the planes in FP32. MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Turquoise Bay AI Pty Ltd. Written here for this
tree's MCDMA wire; no code is copied.

Per row and MoE layer a prompt window sends each expert node its bf16 FFN input row (8,192 bytes at hidden 4,096,
plus the routing) and gets back each node's fp32 partial sum (16,384 bytes). TF_GLM_PARTIALS_BF16=1 (the same on the
attention node and on both expert nodes; unset or 0: every call, header, buffer and byte as before) halves the
replies of prompt windows (MODE_CHUNK, MODE_SLICE, MODE_PAIR, MODE_LANES) to 8,192 bytes a row; decode windows
(MODE_EAGER, 1 .. 128 rows) keep fp32 replies and their bits. It changes outputs (one rounding of each half's sum),
so it is off by default:
- partials_bf16.py: the switch (0 or 1; anything else refuses), a reply row's bytes, the startup refusal, and the
  tests' oracle (round to nearest even from the fp32 bits).
- mcdma.py: Layout(bf16=): rep(rows, prompt) is a prompt window's bf16 reply; room(rows), the reply half the
  mailboxes must hold, is the larger of the bf16 prompt reply and a decode window's fp32 reply of up to EAGER_MOST
  (128) rows, so at 2,048 rows on GLM-5.3-Flash the reply half needs 20 MiB instead of 36 (mib, check_room).
- afd_mcdma.py: McdmaTransport sizes its layout with "partials_bf16", waits for a prompt window's replies at their
  bf16 length and refuses a decode window wider than the reply room; McdmaExpertLink rounds a prompt window's partial
  (fp32, the experts' slot-order sum, unchanged) into its link's own bf16 rows with torch's conversion (to nearest,
  ties to even) and replies with their bytes, on the reply stream after the compute's event over N links
  (TF_GLM_MCDMA_INFLIGHT), else on the compute stream or blocking, as the fp32 reply goes; open_expert_link sizes the
  room with it; sent() gives the wire check the rows the attention adds.
- afd.py: settings_for(partials_bf16=) adds "partials_bf16": 1, which an expert node of an older tree refuses;
  AfdWire.collect lands a prompt window's two bf16 replies in the shared expert's plane of afd_recv, widens them into
  planes 0 and 1 (exact, one kernel) and writes the shared expert's rows after them, so hc_post adds the same three
  fp32 planes in the same order (rank 1's, rank 2's, the shared expert's).
- experts_node.py: serve() reads the node's own TF_GLM_PARTIALS_BF16 before the handshake and refuses to start when
  it differs from the attention node's, naming both settings, under its error key (the attention's watchdog stops
  with it), before its link opens or its weights load; the wire check hashes the rows sent.
- engine.py: the attention node reads the switch before the wire opens, announces it and names it at startup.
- afd_transport.py: the protocol's recv and reply docstrings.
- THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md: "GLM BF16 partial sums on the return wire".

The rounding is row-local (each value alone), so a prompt row's reply is the same in any window: alone or grouped
(TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL), in a pair or lanes, at any number of exchanges in flight.

Tests (CPU): tests/test_glm_partials_bf16.py (24 with the Triton interpreter, 23 without): the setting, the handshake
(an older expert refuses) and the sizes at GLM-5.3-Flash's shapes; the rounding bit for bit against a plain-Python
round to nearest even over every class of value (ties both ways, overflow to infinity, subnormals, signed zeros,
NaN), the widening exact for all 65,536 bf16 patterns, the error within half a bf16 step, the rounding row-local; the
real AfdWire, McdmaTransport and both experts' loops over the fake MCDMA pair, CPU-, GPU-driven and mixed: prompt
chunks, a layer slice, a pair and lanes of 3 and 4 come back as the bf16 rounding of each expert's partial and decode
windows as the partial itself, each reply word 2 or 4 bytes a value, the mailbox holding the bf16 bytes; a row's
reply the same in any window; the planes equal to the switch-off planes with the halves rounded, and glue.hc_post
(interpreted) adding them in rank order in fp32; lanes at 1 to 4 exchanges in flight equal to each lane alone; the
wire check with 0 mismatches; the expert's reply stream with bf16 rows (faked streams and events); the mailbox room at
20 + 36 and 20 + 20 MiB on both ends; the startup refusals (a switch that differs on either side, a bad value) and
serve() with both on; the switch off giving today's sizes, replies and reply lengths.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

