+## GLM BF16 partial sums on the return wire (TF_GLM_PARTIALS_BF16)
+
+Hugh Madden ([@dangerm00se](https://x.com/dangerm00se), [hughmadden](https://github.com/hughmadden)) authored the
+BF16 return path of the AFD design that `families/glm5_next/cuda/partials_bf16.py`, `mcdma.py`, `afd_mcdma.py` and
+`afd.py` bring to this tree's attention/expert split: in
+[glm53f-afd](https://github.com/hughmadden/glm53f-afd/tree/91db3cc6fe672e2724efa3464f63bd31493f63f6)
+(`crates/glm53f-rank/README.md`: the large-M path's reduce step and the return-path table, "Four BF16 planes, added in
+FP32 by the coordinator") each expert rank reduces its routed slots in FP32, rounds the sum to BF16 and returns it,
+and the coordinator adds the planes in FP32. MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Turquoise Bay AI Pty Ltd (the MIT text
+above). No code is copied: here each expert node rounds its half's fp32 partial with torch's float-to-bf16 conversion
+(to nearest, ties to even) for prompt windows only (decode windows keep fp32 replies), the reply's place in the MCDMA
+mailbox is this tree's, and the attention node widens the rows into the fp32 planes `glue.hc_post` adds as before.
+Off unless set (`TF_GLM_PARTIALS_BF16=1`, the same on the attention node and on both expert nodes; it changes
+outputs).
+
 ## GLM attention-side prompt kernels (TF_GLM_LATENT_MMA, TF_GLM_HC_MMA, TF_GLM_SEQ_ROWS, TF_GLM_SPARSE_ONEPASS, TF_GLM_PROMPT_DSA)
 
